docs/project/flagged-performance-results-2026-10-20.md:348:AM's representative estimated V8 allocations are **26.7 MiB/tree**, **202.1 MiB/mesh**, **5.8 MiB/sequence** and **57.6 MiB/class** per SVG call. These expose much larger JS allocation than several peers, but V8 estimates omit Merman native heaps and Rust/WASM linear memory and cannot rank total memory across them. In this particular fresh-process sequence, AM SVG final RSS is about **487.9 MiB**, compared with Beautiful **289.0**, Zombie **271.9**, Pele **146.7**, Merman sync/async **77.0/86.3** and Rust/WASM **163.6 MiB**. RSS is resident memory after the last workload, without a forced-GC checkpoint; it is not peak or a per-input allocation total. The terminal RSS order differs (Beautiful about **637.7 MiB**, AM **279.6 MiB**), reinforcing the need to name the workload/output scope. Full browser Dart memory and packed payloads are reported separately below.
docs/project/flagged-performance-results-2026-10-20.md:360:Each compared input records five timing repetitions per round. The corpus ratio uses the same 345 matched successful inputs in each round: corresponding repetition totals are aggregated across inputs and then the median of five corpus passes is taken, rather than summing independently selected per-input medians. Scaling/agent groups remain separate workloads. Ratios are candidate TF / baseline, paired per round then medianed:
docs/project/flagged-performance-results-2026-10-20.md:388:Release memory is **one fresh worker per engine/group**, not three memory replications. After a loading pass and separate per-input probes, a settled whole-corpus pass measures estimated V8 allocation, an observed start/end/pre-GC heap high-water and forced-GC retained growth. `peakRssBytes` is the OS high-water for the whole worker lifecycle, including its earlier passes; it is not attributable only to the measured pass. Whole-pass allocation is **not the sum of separately probed per-input allocations**.
docs/project/flagged-performance-results-2026-10-20.md:473:Dart is smaller than AM's complete IIFE and this focused SVG consumer in both raw and gzip bytes. AM's lazy first-flow delivery is smaller in gzip than the complete Dart wrapper (**703,266 versus 818,979 bytes**), but it uses 27 requests and has a different delivery/API scope; the 5,468-byte loader alone is not the renderer's download cost. The focused SVG consumer excludes optional sketch backends, terminal output and typed editing; it is a consumer build rather than another shipped complete IIFE. These are offline per-file compressed JS budgets, not complete HTTP wire bytes, app assets, network loading time, npm archive sizes, native Flutter binaries or the full demo app. Optional features do not demonstrate a bundle-size uplift; they retain a measured delivery cost. Exact file-level proof is `review/c517-payload-proof.json`.
docs/project/flagged-performance-results-2026-10-19.md:179:The corpus uses same-rep sums over the 345 matched inputs, then a median within each process and a median of paired round ratios. The paired corpus ratios are 1.113, 0.746, 0.958; median 0.958. Separately summing per-input medians gives different totals; those must not be divided to replace the paired score.
docs/project/flagged-performance-results-2026-10-19.md:188:These memory observations use one separate fresh worker per engine/group, restricted to the matched population; the three rounds replicate timing, not these memory checkpoints. Whole-pass allocation fell about 4.0%; separately GC-probed per-input totals fell about 4.5%, and are a different experiment. Heap peak and RSS did not fall. Retained growth after one pass is not a proof about long-running leaks.
